1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:05,033
Hi, this is a video on the Stop Killing
Games campaign I've been doing,

2
00:00:05,034 --> 00:00:07,978
specifically the European
Citizens' Initiative.

3
00:00:08,550 --> 00:00:12,966
Now since that's kicked off, I've seen
lots of questions and concerns pop up.

4
00:00:12,967 --> 00:00:15,090
That's probably my fault.

5
00:00:15,090 --> 00:00:18,766
Honestly, there's hundreds of
elements involved with this campaign,

6
00:00:18,766 --> 00:00:22,866
so it becomes a haze in my mind
what I need to include in each video.

7
00:00:22,867 --> 00:00:26,533
So instead, this video
is the whole enchilada.

8
00:00:26,533 --> 00:00:29,933
I'm gonna try to answer
every big question I've seen.

9
00:00:29,933 --> 00:00:32,433
I don't want anyone to
be against this campaign

10
00:00:32,434 --> 00:00:35,533
because they feel like they
don't have enough information.

11
00:00:35,534 --> 00:00:38,633
Now if you're already on
board with this initiative,

12
00:00:38,634 --> 00:00:40,900
or if you're completely against it,

13
00:00:40,901 --> 00:00:43,333
then you probably
don't need to watch this.

14
00:00:43,333 --> 00:00:47,366
In fact, I don't recommend it. It's
probably gonna be long and boring.

15
00:00:47,366 --> 00:00:50,966
This is more for people who
really want to drill into the details.

16
00:00:50,967 --> 00:00:54,866
Now if you're watching this, I'm assuming
you've watched one of these videos already,

17
00:00:54,867 --> 00:00:57,466
but if not, super quick recap,

18
00:00:57,467 --> 00:01:01,300
we've launched a European
Citizens' Initiative that, if passed,

19
00:01:01,301 --> 00:01:05,276
would allow customers to retain
working copies of games they've paid for.

20
00:01:05,276 --> 00:01:06,800
Mostly full stop.

21
00:01:07,306 --> 00:01:12,116
It's more complicated than that, but
that's what this and those videos are for.

22
00:01:12,116 --> 00:01:15,400
Oh, and one point that maybe
didn't stick the first time,

23
00:01:15,401 --> 00:01:17,933
we're the only game
in town on this.

24
00:01:17,934 --> 00:01:22,556
So if the practice of destroying video
games bothers you and you're an EU citizen,

25
00:01:22,556 --> 00:01:25,700
this is your only chance
to do something about it.

26
00:01:25,700 --> 00:01:27,950
If you're waiting for something
better to come along,

27
00:01:27,951 --> 00:01:30,333
you'll die of old age
before that happens.

28
00:01:30,334 --> 00:01:32,014
It's this or nothing.

29
00:01:32,200 --> 00:01:34,867
Maybe you don't
believe me on that, fine,

30
00:01:35,201 --> 00:01:40,400
but if this fails in 10 years, 20
years, you realize, "Oh, huh."

31
00:01:40,401 --> 00:01:44,733
"I guess Stop Killing Games really
was the last stand on that, huh?"

32
00:01:44,734 --> 00:01:47,633
Okay, I'm gonna get to the
questions, but before I do,

33
00:01:47,633 --> 00:01:51,400
there's a few core concepts that
are gonna come up again and again,

34
00:01:51,401 --> 00:01:53,366
and might help
frame all of this.

35
00:01:53,391 --> 00:01:58,233
Concept number one: always
ask, "What's the alternative?"

36
00:01:58,233 --> 00:02:00,466
I don't know if I've
communicated this well,

37
00:02:00,467 --> 00:02:03,500
but I'm actually in
favor of any solution

38
00:02:03,501 --> 00:02:06,500
that solves the problem of
video games being destroyed.

39
00:02:06,500 --> 00:02:09,300
So when I hear
criticisms of our plan,

40
00:02:09,301 --> 00:02:14,966
my first thought is always, "What's the
alternative solution that saves video games?"

41
00:02:14,966 --> 00:02:17,100
If somebody has an
alternate solution,

42
00:02:17,101 --> 00:02:20,900
cool, we're on the same team,
then we're just discussing tactics.

43
00:02:20,901 --> 00:02:26,100
But if somebody does not have an alternate
solution, but they're against our solution,

44
00:02:26,100 --> 00:02:29,366
well, then they're
against all solutions.

45
00:02:29,366 --> 00:02:34,300
So deductively, that means they are in favor
of perpetuating the destruction of games,

46
00:02:34,301 --> 00:02:36,533
which means they're
the opposition.

47
00:02:36,533 --> 00:02:39,900
In which case, we don't
really value their critique

48
00:02:39,901 --> 00:02:42,393
because they don't want
to solve the problem.

49
00:02:42,393 --> 00:02:46,466
Like if a parent says to a kid, "Clean
your room. I don't care how you do it",

50
00:02:46,467 --> 00:02:48,173
and the kid says, "No!"

51
00:02:48,173 --> 00:02:51,033
well, then the parent might
clean the room for the kid,

52
00:02:51,034 --> 00:02:53,300
and throw out some stuff
they didn't want them to,

53
00:02:53,301 --> 00:02:55,933
because the kid refused
to solve the problem.

54
00:02:55,934 --> 00:02:57,933
So anything about our
plans you don't like,

55
00:02:57,934 --> 00:03:01,633
I encourage everyone to
ask, "What's the alternative?"

56
00:03:01,634 --> 00:03:04,843
It's a fast way to figure
out who's on what side.

57
00:03:04,843 --> 00:03:08,000
And some people may not
even realize that's their stance

58
00:03:08,001 --> 00:03:10,200
until they go
through that process.

59
00:03:10,201 --> 00:03:12,200
I mean, they might
have good intentions,

60
00:03:12,201 --> 00:03:16,300
but good intentions and no
action, what does that get you?

61
00:03:16,300 --> 00:03:18,023
All right, just a couple more.

62
00:03:18,023 --> 00:03:22,933
Concept two: most of what we're
doing is about future games.

63
00:03:22,933 --> 00:03:26,093
For existing games, don't
worry. We'll get to that.

64
00:03:26,093 --> 00:03:30,693
But the point is, this campaign is focused
on games that don't even exist yet.

65
00:03:30,693 --> 00:03:34,100
So when I see comments saying
what we're asking for is impossible,

66
00:03:34,101 --> 00:03:36,066
or we don't know what
we're talking about,

67
00:03:36,066 --> 00:03:40,986
what I hear is somebody saying
"It is impossible or impractical"

68
00:03:40,987 --> 00:03:45,686
"to make an online game in the
future with an end-of-life plan."

69
00:03:45,686 --> 00:03:50,746
Now, I and many developers I've talked
to think that's a pretty silly statement,

70
00:03:50,747 --> 00:03:54,346
but I've seen so many
comments along those lines.

71
00:03:54,346 --> 00:03:57,133
I think a few out there have
an almost myopic focus

72
00:03:57,134 --> 00:04:00,266
on games right now and
how things can't change,

73
00:04:00,267 --> 00:04:02,467
and that's not
where our focus is.

74
00:04:03,066 --> 00:04:05,333
Third concept. Almost done.

75
00:04:05,334 --> 00:04:10,200
It is impossible to save games
without SOME effort from developers

76
00:04:10,201 --> 00:04:12,601
and SOME disruption to the industry.

77
00:04:13,000 --> 00:04:15,700
The video game industry has
been going down the wrong road

78
00:04:15,700 --> 00:04:18,133
on game preservation
for a long time.

79
00:04:18,134 --> 00:04:20,020
There's so much damage already.

80
00:04:20,020 --> 00:04:23,966
Some out there think that the second
there's any inconvenience to the industry,

81
00:04:23,967 --> 00:04:27,566
no matter how small, then
this entire plan is unfeasible.

82
00:04:27,566 --> 00:04:30,566
It kind of reminds me of
car manufacturers in the 60s

83
00:04:30,567 --> 00:04:33,386
when they were required
to add seatbelts to cars.

84
00:04:33,386 --> 00:04:38,306
Car companies protested that up and
down and fought it every step of the way.

85
00:04:38,306 --> 00:04:43,476
And you know what? Adding seatbelts did
add to the cost and work of making a car.

86
00:04:43,476 --> 00:04:46,612
But how much, especially
now that time has passed?

87
00:04:47,440 --> 00:04:49,800
I would guess less than 1%.

88
00:04:50,366 --> 00:04:55,866
I think it could be similar for video
games of the future. Remember Concept 2.

89
00:04:56,586 --> 00:04:58,866
And the last core concept,

90
00:04:59,346 --> 00:05:02,936
the European Citizens'
Initiative is not final law.

91
00:05:02,936 --> 00:05:05,944
Something about it will
change. It's inevitable.

92
00:05:06,000 --> 00:05:10,306
What we're trying to do is aim the arrow
the best we can and hope it hits the mark.

93
00:05:10,306 --> 00:05:12,336
Okay, questions.

94
00:05:12,336 --> 00:05:15,786
Will the law we're proposing be retroactive?

95
00:05:15,786 --> 00:05:21,076
No, it couldn't be even if we wanted it to.
Sorry if that wasn't clear earlier.

96
00:05:21,076 --> 00:05:23,786
Isn't your initiative too vague?

97
00:05:23,786 --> 00:05:28,176
There's a few layers to this question.
Like, remember Concept 4.

98
00:05:28,176 --> 00:05:31,233
And it's not actually
supposed to be final law.

99
00:05:31,234 --> 00:05:34,096
Like, did you know
there's a word limit to it?

100
00:05:34,096 --> 00:05:38,566
It's supposed to be a basic
explanation and your reasons for it.

101
00:05:38,566 --> 00:05:41,766
And you have to cite
which EU directives apply

102
00:05:41,767 --> 00:05:44,026
to show that the EU
has authority for it.

103
00:05:44,026 --> 00:05:47,034
Oh, yeah, that was a
lot of fun doing that part.

104
00:05:47,036 --> 00:05:50,366
Also, have you looked at
any of the other initiatives?

105
00:05:50,367 --> 00:05:52,733
This one: "Tax the rich."

106
00:05:52,734 --> 00:05:57,633
This initiative does not give a
number as to what qualifies as "rich".

107
00:05:57,633 --> 00:06:01,366
Or this one, creation of
an "environmental authority".

108
00:06:01,366 --> 00:06:03,876
That's talking about
creating a new agency.

109
00:06:03,876 --> 00:06:08,066
Do you have any idea how many
rules and regulations that would take?

110
00:06:08,066 --> 00:06:09,799
That would be books worth.

111
00:06:10,086 --> 00:06:13,376
So we could be more specific,
but it wouldn't matter.

112
00:06:13,376 --> 00:06:16,816
That's not our job. That's
the EU Commission's job.

113
00:06:16,816 --> 00:06:21,926
In some ways, this slide I posted is
more important than the initiative itself,

114
00:06:21,926 --> 00:06:25,611
because this roughly reflects
the views of the organizers,

115
00:06:25,611 --> 00:06:29,296
the people who would be going to
Brussels to discuss this with the EU.

116
00:06:29,296 --> 00:06:33,633
Another layer to this is the law
itself can be vague or broad.

117
00:06:34,151 --> 00:06:39,296
Like, you know, police officers, they have to
have "reasonable suspicion" to stop someone.

118
00:06:39,296 --> 00:06:42,936
And they need "probable
cause" to arrest someone.

119
00:06:42,936 --> 00:06:44,386
What does that mean?

120
00:06:44,386 --> 00:06:46,866
Yeah, see, you have to use
some common sense because

121
00:06:46,867 --> 00:06:49,467
the law can't cover
every last scenario.

122
00:06:50,036 --> 00:06:53,636
Like, say an officer sees somebody
who he thinks has weed on him.

123
00:06:53,636 --> 00:06:55,826
But it turns out it's
just a bag of oregano.

124
00:06:55,826 --> 00:06:59,936
Do we need to write a law saying
that if you're holding a bag of oregano,

125
00:06:59,936 --> 00:07:02,603
then that counts as
reasonable suspicion?

126
00:07:02,946 --> 00:07:07,026
Well, what if we forgot to write
a law for a bag of dried parsley?

127
00:07:07,026 --> 00:07:09,236
See, it just doesn't work.

128
00:07:09,236 --> 00:07:11,346
And it's the same for games.

129
00:07:11,346 --> 00:07:13,433
Like in the initiative, some
were asking what is meant

130
00:07:13,434 --> 00:07:16,626
by a "reasonably functional"
or "playable" state.

131
00:07:16,626 --> 00:07:21,666
Well, if it's a racing game, I would think
that means you race cars in the game world.

132
00:07:21,666 --> 00:07:23,166
If it's an arena shooter,

133
00:07:23,167 --> 00:07:26,976
I would think that means you enter
game maps and shoot at other players.

134
00:07:26,976 --> 00:07:32,361
If it's an RPG, boy, that could
mean fight monsters, talk to NPCs,

135
00:07:32,361 --> 00:07:35,906
use items, manage your
inventory, lots of stuff.

136
00:07:35,906 --> 00:07:41,506
But what if we try to be specific and
make the law race cars in the game world?

137
00:07:41,506 --> 00:07:45,826
Well, does that work for the
arena shooter or the RPG?

138
00:07:45,826 --> 00:07:48,466
I mean, I guess a few
of them, but not most.

139
00:07:48,466 --> 00:07:50,000
It just won't work.

140
00:07:50,001 --> 00:07:52,231
Same goes for technology.

141
00:07:52,231 --> 00:07:57,616
What works code-wise for an arena
shooter probably doesn't work for an MMO.

142
00:07:57,616 --> 00:07:59,886
That's why we leave
it to the developer.

143
00:07:59,886 --> 00:08:02,176
Some were
complaining about that.

144
00:08:02,176 --> 00:08:05,558
Well, the alternative would
be to mandate exactly how

145
00:08:05,558 --> 00:08:08,822
every company writes the
code that fixes their game.

146
00:08:08,877 --> 00:08:12,396
Not only is that unrealistic,
nobody wants that.

147
00:08:12,396 --> 00:08:18,000
Specific rules do not work for all
games, so you have to be broad.

148
00:08:18,606 --> 00:08:21,776
Doesn't your initiative still go too far?

149
00:08:21,776 --> 00:08:25,906
Okay, we just covered why we have
to be broad and can't be too specific,

150
00:08:25,906 --> 00:08:31,746
but there is a 0% chance this initiative
survives with everything we're asking for.

151
00:08:31,746 --> 00:08:33,856
There's going to be industry input.

152
00:08:33,856 --> 00:08:36,146
In fact, I'm kind of
worried about one of them.

153
00:08:36,146 --> 00:08:40,816
One of our members found out about a
lobbying group, videogameseurope.eu.

154
00:08:40,816 --> 00:08:42,333
If you look at the
membership list,

155
00:08:42,334 --> 00:08:45,726
there are a lot of companies
with live service games on here.

156
00:08:45,726 --> 00:08:49,616
I would feel so much better
if CD Projekt was on this list.

157
00:08:49,616 --> 00:08:54,306
You know, a major European game developer
that gives a damn about preservation.

158
00:08:54,306 --> 00:08:56,866
Back to the initiative,
it's going to change,

159
00:08:56,866 --> 00:09:01,166
so we need to aim a little higher to hit the
mark so we have some negotiating leverage.

160
00:09:01,166 --> 00:09:04,871
Okay, I am not speaking for the
initiative members in this next part.

161
00:09:04,871 --> 00:09:09,056
It's not a hive mind. Some agree with
me, some are more radical than me.

162
00:09:09,056 --> 00:09:12,926
But here are some compromises
I personally might agree to.

163
00:09:12,926 --> 00:09:16,096
Instead of insisting that the
game works on shutdown,

164
00:09:16,096 --> 00:09:21,216
have a "best effort" from the developers
so that savvy users could "repair" the game.

165
00:09:21,216 --> 00:09:26,476
This could be server binaries, server source
code, though again, this is optional, guys.

166
00:09:26,477 --> 00:09:29,796
That would not be the only option.
I'm not mandating that.

167
00:09:29,796 --> 00:09:33,586
Or if you want to be really
generous, repair instructions.

168
00:09:33,586 --> 00:09:36,066
Here, I'll pull up my old
slide from a while back.

169
00:09:36,066 --> 00:09:38,126
This would kind of
be the bare minimum.

170
00:09:38,126 --> 00:09:39,916
Okay, back to compromises.

171
00:09:39,917 --> 00:09:44,416
Not include subscription service
games. I'll get into that one later.

172
00:09:44,416 --> 00:09:48,176
And finally, grandfather
in existing games.

173
00:09:48,176 --> 00:09:50,656
That would be a huge compromise.

174
00:09:50,656 --> 00:09:53,856
Because every game that's
grandfathered in will die.

175
00:09:53,856 --> 00:09:56,816
Or 98% of them,
something around there.

176
00:09:56,816 --> 00:10:01,136
A higher percentage of those games would
survive if they were not grandfathered in.

177
00:10:01,136 --> 00:10:03,856
So that's giving up hundreds
of games right there.

178
00:10:03,856 --> 00:10:05,456
It would be a devil's bargain.

179
00:10:05,456 --> 00:10:09,346
Hundreds of games sacrificed to
save almost all games in the future.

180
00:10:09,346 --> 00:10:12,176
And this is about as far as
the compromises could go.

181
00:10:12,176 --> 00:10:16,386
If we bargain below this, there's
basically no point to any of this.

182
00:10:16,386 --> 00:10:19,696
Because then there would be so little
protection, no games would be saved.

183
00:10:19,696 --> 00:10:21,726
So we have to have some buffer.

184
00:10:21,726 --> 00:10:26,556
Under your initiative, do all features
need to work when the game is shut down?

185
00:10:26,556 --> 00:10:29,576
This is another point a
few members disagree on.

186
00:10:29,576 --> 00:10:33,016
Me, I'd say no, but
it's kind of a spectrum.

187
00:10:33,016 --> 00:10:36,362
We have that phrase
"reasonably playable state".

188
00:10:36,362 --> 00:10:40,116
So let's say you have an online
arena shooter, it shuts down,

189
00:10:40,116 --> 00:10:42,366
the company releases
an end-of-life patch,

190
00:10:42,366 --> 00:10:47,436
and everything works except for
achievements and matchmaking.

191
00:10:47,436 --> 00:10:52,233
Well, I think most people would say, "Yeah,
good enough. The game's saved. It works."

192
00:10:52,234 --> 00:10:54,183
But then let's say you
had another game,

193
00:10:54,184 --> 00:10:57,906
it shuts down, and you
can technically start it,

194
00:10:57,906 --> 00:11:01,676
but all you can do is get to the main
menu and nothing works past that.

195
00:11:01,676 --> 00:11:03,700
Well, in that other game,
I think most people

196
00:11:03,701 --> 00:11:07,093
would not consider that a
"reasonably playable state".

197
00:11:07,176 --> 00:11:11,076
So that game might face a higher
chance of receiving complaints

198
00:11:11,076 --> 00:11:13,586
to the Consumer Protection
Agency in that country.

199
00:11:13,586 --> 00:11:16,576
So I can't tell you exactly
where the dividing line is,

200
00:11:16,576 --> 00:11:20,776
but the more the game works, the more
it's likely to be an acceptable solution.

201
00:11:20,776 --> 00:11:24,436
Do you expect companies to
keep running servers forever?

202
00:11:24,436 --> 00:11:28,933
No. They can end support and
turn off servers whenever they want.

203
00:11:29,386 --> 00:11:32,096
But they have to do it
in a responsible way,

204
00:11:32,096 --> 00:11:36,673
like "Gran Turismo Sport",
"Knockout City", "Duelyst", and so on.

205
00:11:37,267 --> 00:11:40,133
What about games that don't
require a connection to the publisher

206
00:11:40,134 --> 00:11:43,933
or DRM servers, et cetera,
when support ends to work?

207
00:11:44,603 --> 00:11:48,386
Nothing. Those kinds of games
would be exempt from all of this.

208
00:11:48,386 --> 00:11:50,853
And that actually is
most video games.

209
00:11:51,663 --> 00:11:55,033
Are any people involved
with this initiative developers?

210
00:11:55,033 --> 00:11:59,500
Yes, a few are game or server
software developers in the initiative.

211
00:11:59,883 --> 00:12:03,233
I've also had a lot of game developers
reach out to me in support of this,

212
00:12:03,234 --> 00:12:05,100
mostly indie ones.

213
00:12:05,101 --> 00:12:08,066
I think our highest-profile game
developer in support of this

214
00:12:08,067 --> 00:12:12,093
might be Running With Scissors,
the developer of the "Postal" games.

215
00:12:12,093 --> 00:12:15,333
["Hi there, would you
like to sign my petition?"]

216
00:12:15,334 --> 00:12:19,622
I imagine they understand the
madness we're facing better than most.

217
00:12:19,900 --> 00:12:23,033
And finally, there's been a couple
developers at large companies

218
00:12:23,034 --> 00:12:25,901
that wish to remain
anonymous, so they will.

219
00:12:27,000 --> 00:12:32,376
I'm a developer with an online-only game.
What will happen if this initiative passes?

220
00:12:32,833 --> 00:12:36,100
Okay, first off, I can't
answer this question 100%,

221
00:12:36,101 --> 00:12:39,900
because, back to point
four, things will change.

222
00:12:39,900 --> 00:12:42,830
So a lot of this depends
on what the EU decides.

223
00:12:42,830 --> 00:12:45,180
We'll start with existing games.

224
00:12:45,180 --> 00:12:48,835
You may have several options,
though most of them are not ideal.

225
00:12:48,835 --> 00:12:51,700
But remember point
one and point three.

226
00:12:52,533 --> 00:12:53,766
Option one:

227
00:12:53,767 --> 00:12:57,500
you can come up with an end-of-life
plan to keep it working after shutdown.

228
00:12:57,500 --> 00:12:59,633
Though that may not be possible.

229
00:12:59,940 --> 00:13:04,700
See, you could have licenses and middleware
that you don't have permission to distribute.

230
00:13:04,700 --> 00:13:07,993
Or you know what? Maybe you just
don't want to work on the game anymore.

231
00:13:07,993 --> 00:13:09,193
I get it.

232
00:13:09,270 --> 00:13:13,878
Option two: you could shut down the
game before the law went into effect.

233
00:13:13,970 --> 00:13:15,666
Yes, that sucks.

234
00:13:15,666 --> 00:13:17,578
But you think it doesn't
suck for the consumer

235
00:13:17,578 --> 00:13:20,210
every time every other
game gets shut down?

236
00:13:20,210 --> 00:13:22,730
And the quick-to-react
people out there might say,

237
00:13:22,730 --> 00:13:25,601
"Oh look, this proposal
is going to kill games!"

238
00:13:25,601 --> 00:13:30,200
You mean games that were on death
row and were going to get killed anyway?

239
00:13:30,200 --> 00:13:33,180
So yes, this would
move the timetable up,

240
00:13:33,180 --> 00:13:36,252
but the aftermath would
be everything gets saved.

241
00:13:36,460 --> 00:13:40,633
Option three, maybe--
this one depends on the EU.

242
00:13:40,633 --> 00:13:43,533
You can move to a
subscription model instead.

243
00:13:43,980 --> 00:13:48,266
Now this is probably a losing
proposition in the long term, we know.

244
00:13:48,266 --> 00:13:53,610
But then at least customers are informed how
much time they have left at any given time.

245
00:13:53,610 --> 00:13:55,933
Option four, another maybe.

246
00:13:56,200 --> 00:14:01,064
Make a best effort to let the customer
repair or run the game after shutdown.

247
00:14:01,780 --> 00:14:05,190
So this could be server binaries,
maybe server source code.

248
00:14:05,191 --> 00:14:07,533
Again, not mandatory.

249
00:14:07,533 --> 00:14:10,940
Releasing packet documentation,
I'll bring up that slide again.

250
00:14:10,940 --> 00:14:13,130
Removing DRM and so on.

251
00:14:13,130 --> 00:14:15,900
Something to give users a fighting chance.

252
00:14:15,900 --> 00:14:18,700
And option five, another maybe.

253
00:14:18,700 --> 00:14:22,700
All games get grandfathered in and
none of this applies to existing games.

254
00:14:22,700 --> 00:14:24,600
This would kill the most games,

255
00:14:24,601 --> 00:14:27,720
but this is probably the option
most developers would want.

256
00:14:27,720 --> 00:14:29,500
Now that's for existing games.

257
00:14:29,500 --> 00:14:34,566
For future games, it's everything I just
mentioned, which have multiple maybes.

258
00:14:34,567 --> 00:14:39,623
Except for grandfathering and shutting
down before the law is active, obviously.

259
00:14:39,728 --> 00:14:43,967
I realize this is less than ideal,
but remember point three.

260
00:14:43,968 --> 00:14:48,800
We have to break the loop of games
being designed with no end-of-life plan.

261
00:14:48,800 --> 00:14:53,600
Otherwise, this excuse gets perpetuated
forever and progress becomes impossible.

262
00:14:53,600 --> 00:14:56,400
Now, one thing I think some
developers aren't considering

263
00:14:56,400 --> 00:14:59,440
is if this passes and it
becomes a known requirement,

264
00:14:59,440 --> 00:15:02,530
I think there's going to be a gold
rush for middleware developers

265
00:15:02,530 --> 00:15:06,710
to come up with easy solutions to
create an end-of-life build for your game.

266
00:15:06,710 --> 00:15:09,533
The market forces
will be enormous.

267
00:15:09,533 --> 00:15:13,033
I'm trying to keep it real.
This is not an ideal situation,

268
00:15:13,034 --> 00:15:17,194
and we actually want this to be
as easy as possible on developers.

269
00:15:17,360 --> 00:15:22,400
But remember point one, what's the
alternative that still saves games?

270
00:15:22,667 --> 00:15:27,201
I don't know how this works without
some compromise from the developers.

271
00:15:27,201 --> 00:15:29,166
If the initiative passes,

272
00:15:29,167 --> 00:15:31,866
what would developers have
to change for future games

273
00:15:31,867 --> 00:15:33,800
while the game was being supported?

274
00:15:33,800 --> 00:15:36,410
Well, visibly, absolutely nothing.

275
00:15:36,410 --> 00:15:39,546
Though you really should
have an end-of-life plan.

276
00:15:39,930 --> 00:15:42,410
Other than that, it's
business as usual.

277
00:15:42,410 --> 00:15:46,442
You can still have microtransactions,
loot boxes, DLC, whatever.

278
00:15:47,185 --> 00:15:50,533
Would this initiative
affect subscription games?

279
00:15:51,000 --> 00:15:54,600
Well, that's another question that
depends on what the EU says.

280
00:15:54,600 --> 00:15:57,066
Personally, I think it's very unlikely

281
00:15:57,066 --> 00:16:01,100
because that doesn't fit well with
other existing consumer laws.

282
00:16:01,100 --> 00:16:04,371
I think the only way you could
even make that argument

283
00:16:04,371 --> 00:16:06,991
would be that this is
necessary for preservation

284
00:16:06,991 --> 00:16:10,491
and most governments don't
seem to care about that at all.

285
00:16:10,491 --> 00:16:13,171
However, I don't think
this is a huge loss,

286
00:16:13,171 --> 00:16:16,499
since only a handful of
games operate that way today.

287
00:16:16,961 --> 00:16:22,021
So if we can give up those but
then save 99% of other games,

288
00:16:22,021 --> 00:16:23,931
I'm willing to
make that bargain.

289
00:16:23,931 --> 00:16:27,700
Won't lots of games turn
subscription-only if this passes?

290
00:16:27,700 --> 00:16:30,051
Well, first, I think
that's very doubtful.

291
00:16:30,051 --> 00:16:34,533
But if this were to happen, I'm not
sure you could even blame this on us.

292
00:16:34,533 --> 00:16:37,000
The thing is, we've been here before.

293
00:16:37,111 --> 00:16:41,900
In the 2000s, lots of publishers were trying
to cash in on the success of "EverQuest"

294
00:16:41,901 --> 00:16:44,101
and especially "World of Warcraft".

295
00:16:44,531 --> 00:16:49,651
There were MMOs popping up left and
right with subscription fees, dozens of them.

296
00:16:49,911 --> 00:16:53,495
Almost all of those either
died out or went free to play.

297
00:16:53,821 --> 00:16:56,340
It turns out a lot of
people don't like having to

298
00:16:56,341 --> 00:16:59,349
pay money every single
month, for several games.

299
00:16:59,601 --> 00:17:01,281
The market's only so big.

300
00:17:01,631 --> 00:17:05,087
Nowadays, it's mostly just
the old guard that survived.

301
00:17:05,151 --> 00:17:10,061
Like "World of Warcraft", "Final
Fantasy" games, "EVE Online".

302
00:17:10,211 --> 00:17:14,721
The market for subscription games is
only so big and publishers know this.

303
00:17:14,721 --> 00:17:18,311
Otherwise, they'd be cramming these
subscription games down our mouth.

304
00:17:18,311 --> 00:17:23,233
Speaking of which, this is
already happening or trying to.

305
00:17:23,741 --> 00:17:26,541
EA and Ubisoft have subscription services.

306
00:17:26,931 --> 00:17:29,500
PlayStation Now, Game Pass.

307
00:17:29,501 --> 00:17:33,900
Stadia died, thankfully,
and it had exclusives.

308
00:17:33,901 --> 00:17:36,733
Well, what happens when these
other subscription services decide,

309
00:17:36,733 --> 00:17:40,954
"Hey, we should have exclusives
too, that you HAVE to rent to play".

310
00:17:40,954 --> 00:17:45,433
We're already on this trajectory,
so there's not a lot to lose here.

311
00:17:45,773 --> 00:17:49,463
But I don't think we're going to
lose much on these lines anyway.

312
00:17:49,463 --> 00:17:52,493
You're talking mostly
about "live service" games.

313
00:17:52,493 --> 00:17:55,853
Isn't it unreasonable to
expect to own a service?

314
00:17:55,853 --> 00:17:59,300
Oh boy, this is going to be a long
answer. This one gets into the weeds.

315
00:17:59,300 --> 00:18:02,100
First, let's talk about
the legal answer to this.

316
00:18:02,100 --> 00:18:05,167
In Europe, the United States, forget it.

317
00:18:05,783 --> 00:18:07,500
Subscription-based games,

318
00:18:07,501 --> 00:18:10,413
I don't think we have a leg to
stand on in regard to commerce law.

319
00:18:10,413 --> 00:18:14,061
So that's "World of Warcraft",
"Final Fantasy XIV", and so on.

320
00:18:14,533 --> 00:18:20,666
These games tell you exactly when they
end, and that's what normal services do.

321
00:18:20,933 --> 00:18:23,384
So yeah, I don't think there's
anything in the law that gets us there,

322
00:18:23,384 --> 00:18:26,900
and as I mentioned earlier,
I also think it's a hard ask.

323
00:18:26,900 --> 00:18:29,033
But what about other
live service games?

324
00:18:29,033 --> 00:18:32,253
Ones that you buy
once, then that's it.

325
00:18:32,253 --> 00:18:36,633
So games like "The Crew",
"Destiny", "Overwatch".

326
00:18:36,634 --> 00:18:41,300
It is not clear in multiple countries
if those are goods or services.

327
00:18:41,643 --> 00:18:44,466
You pay a one-time
fee for a product,

328
00:18:44,466 --> 00:18:46,766
with no stated expiration date,

329
00:18:46,767 --> 00:18:48,501
that sounds like a "good".

330
00:18:48,766 --> 00:18:54,599
But it depends on an external service,
except you're not told how long that lasts.

331
00:18:54,986 --> 00:18:59,516
So that doesn't really meet the full
definition of a "service" commercially either.

332
00:18:59,666 --> 00:19:02,610
Now you can buy lifetime
memberships to things,

333
00:19:02,786 --> 00:19:06,114
but those are associated
with the business operating.

334
00:19:06,146 --> 00:19:11,599
So if I bought a lifetime membership to
a gym, then it's canceled after four years,

335
00:19:11,771 --> 00:19:16,899
but the gym is still operating and
trying to sell me a new lifetime plan?

336
00:19:17,356 --> 00:19:20,256
I'd be pissed. I think
you could sue for that.

337
00:19:20,326 --> 00:19:25,766
"The Culling II", a one-time purchase,
lasted eight days before it shut down.

338
00:19:26,096 --> 00:19:31,176
"Guild Wars", also a one-time purchase,
has been going for over 19 years.

339
00:19:31,633 --> 00:19:35,966
So how can customers make an
informed decision with that kind of spread?

340
00:19:35,966 --> 00:19:39,196
Consumer laws can get
picky about that sort of thing.

341
00:19:39,196 --> 00:19:42,100
If I bought a
warranty for a dryer,

342
00:19:42,433 --> 00:19:46,396
and the company refused to tell
me how long that warranty lasted.

343
00:19:46,396 --> 00:19:50,336
"Maybe it's a week, maybe it's five
years! We're not going to tell you."

344
00:19:50,336 --> 00:19:53,664
How the hell would that
be legal in other industries?

345
00:19:54,276 --> 00:19:57,604
So just because they call
themselves "service games",

346
00:19:57,876 --> 00:20:01,200
doesn't mean they fit the
legal definition of a service.

347
00:20:01,200 --> 00:20:05,071
So that's the legal side, but let's
move into the philosophical side.

348
00:20:05,071 --> 00:20:08,046
I am not aware of
any live service game

349
00:20:08,047 --> 00:20:11,466
that couldn't be converted to
something you couldn't preserve forever

350
00:20:11,467 --> 00:20:13,726
if customers had
access to the code.

351
00:20:13,726 --> 00:20:17,133
Okay, now this here is just my
outlook, but with a service game,

352
00:20:17,133 --> 00:20:21,366
there's stuff I consider a "real
service" and an "artificial service".

353
00:20:21,366 --> 00:20:24,833
The real services take real
people doing something.

354
00:20:25,066 --> 00:20:28,596
So that would be player
moderation, creating new content,

355
00:20:28,596 --> 00:20:35,233
bug fixes, technical support, and the cost
and convenience of running company servers.

356
00:20:35,286 --> 00:20:40,146
There's more. The point is, this all
takes real people actively working.

357
00:20:40,221 --> 00:20:42,546
If they stop, so does the service.

358
00:20:42,696 --> 00:20:46,936
Now the "artificial service" is
being able to run the game.

359
00:20:46,936 --> 00:20:50,381
The only reason it's a service is
because the code is being withheld.

360
00:20:50,381 --> 00:20:55,096
Unlike the other column, no human even
needs to be alive for this part to work,

361
00:20:55,096 --> 00:20:56,806
because the work was already done.

362
00:20:56,806 --> 00:21:01,846
The second the customer has a copy of the
server software, it ceases being a service.

363
00:21:01,846 --> 00:21:03,773
Yeah, I might need
some custom hardware,

364
00:21:03,773 --> 00:21:06,321
but preservationists will
jump through those hoops.

365
00:21:06,321 --> 00:21:08,166
And it's not like
they're freeloaders;

366
00:21:08,166 --> 00:21:11,766
the customers have already paid
the company money for the game!

367
00:21:11,766 --> 00:21:13,786
Sometimes a crapload of money.

368
00:21:13,786 --> 00:21:16,270
And it's insult to injury
that many games will just

369
00:21:16,270 --> 00:21:18,856
give you the server side
as part of your purchase.

370
00:21:18,856 --> 00:21:21,176
So even on a conceptual level,

371
00:21:21,176 --> 00:21:26,586
we are really stretching definitions to
call THIS part of the game a "service".

372
00:21:26,586 --> 00:21:28,066
And let me tell you a secret:

373
00:21:28,166 --> 00:21:30,833
any game can be made
into a service game.

374
00:21:31,256 --> 00:21:32,516
Like take "Super Mario Bros."

375
00:21:32,516 --> 00:21:35,816
You can remove the Goombas
and the power block locations,

376
00:21:35,816 --> 00:21:40,436
hold that data server-side, and then lock
you out in the menu until you authenticate.

377
00:21:40,436 --> 00:21:43,833
Now "Super Mario Bros."
is a "live service game"

378
00:21:43,833 --> 00:21:46,526
that will brick if the
company shuts it down.

379
00:21:46,526 --> 00:21:47,736
Hooray!

380
00:21:47,736 --> 00:21:51,826
All this crap that prevents you
from playing the game is artificial.

381
00:21:51,826 --> 00:21:54,733
Now of course it makes sense
to keep running it as a service,

382
00:21:54,734 --> 00:21:57,016
because you can make
a lot of money that way.

383
00:21:57,016 --> 00:22:01,496
Plus all those real service aspects I
mentioned improve the quality of the game.

384
00:22:01,496 --> 00:22:04,086
And we're not trying
to stop any of that.

385
00:22:04,086 --> 00:22:07,256
Companies can keep making
money as long as they want to.

386
00:22:07,256 --> 00:22:11,416
We're specifically talking about cases
where all the services have shut down,

387
00:22:11,416 --> 00:22:13,816
and the company is no
longer making money.

388
00:22:13,816 --> 00:22:17,186
Now of course there can be many legal
reasons the company doesn't just release

389
00:22:17,187 --> 00:22:20,166
their server software to customers
once they're done making money.

390
00:22:20,166 --> 00:22:24,766
But guess what? That's why
we're trying to get the law changed.

391
00:22:24,986 --> 00:22:27,766
So these barriers don't need to exist.

392
00:22:28,346 --> 00:22:31,856
And this next part I don't know
if I can even explain to everyone.

393
00:22:31,856 --> 00:22:35,886
But art has innate value to
me, which includes games.

394
00:22:36,056 --> 00:22:38,733
I think destroying good art is awful.

395
00:22:39,046 --> 00:22:42,066
Games are code.
They can be preserved.

396
00:22:42,186 --> 00:22:46,486
But we're creating systems where
destruction is the only option possible.

397
00:22:46,486 --> 00:22:48,076
And it's preventable.

398
00:22:48,076 --> 00:22:52,296
Every time MMO emulators get
leaked, or reverse engineered,

399
00:22:52,296 --> 00:22:57,033
or released by the devs themselves,
it just shows this is all possible.

400
00:22:57,366 --> 00:23:00,646
I would like to see all games
people care about saved.

401
00:23:00,646 --> 00:23:02,446
Even the subscription ones.

402
00:23:02,486 --> 00:23:05,186
Even though I know I don't
have a legal angle on those.

403
00:23:05,286 --> 00:23:08,276
But other live service
games? Give me a break.

404
00:23:08,276 --> 00:23:13,206
At this point in time, it's not even clear
if those are breaking the law or not.

405
00:23:13,206 --> 00:23:17,558
But even if they're not, customers
hate having their games destroyed.

406
00:23:17,846 --> 00:23:20,536
This is not a hard
concept to understand.

407
00:23:20,536 --> 00:23:22,616
The mindset here is aspirational.

408
00:23:22,616 --> 00:23:25,741
To try and make art and
gaming better than it is now.

409
00:23:25,741 --> 00:23:28,376
Because there's so
much obvious destruction.

410
00:23:28,376 --> 00:23:32,099
Why settle for an awful
practice that might be illegal,

411
00:23:32,216 --> 00:23:35,333
when a better way
hasn't even been tried?

412
00:23:35,696 --> 00:23:38,596
If you don't get it, I
don't think you will.

413
00:23:38,936 --> 00:23:43,233
Isn't there a chance gaming will get
worse because of government involvement?

414
00:23:43,233 --> 00:23:47,233
I think there's a bigger chance it gets
worse WITHOUT government involvement.

415
00:23:47,233 --> 00:23:51,766
Now could they misinterpret things,
or apply this in a clunky way? Sure.

416
00:23:51,766 --> 00:23:53,633
But we don't have much to lose.

417
00:23:53,634 --> 00:23:56,233
From our perspective, there's
nothing worse in gaming

418
00:23:56,234 --> 00:23:58,666
than having games you like destroyed.

419
00:23:58,666 --> 00:24:01,833
This practice has already
destroyed hundreds of games.

420
00:24:01,833 --> 00:24:03,900
We can see where this is going.

421
00:24:03,900 --> 00:24:07,633
What if in the future 90% of games are
online only, and they just keep killing them?

422
00:24:07,633 --> 00:24:09,263
They're coming for all your crap.

423
00:24:09,263 --> 00:24:12,543
The industry refuses to solve
this problem. It wants more of it.

424
00:24:12,543 --> 00:24:14,563
Government's the only option left.

425
00:24:14,563 --> 00:24:17,500
You think I want to be
doing this? Any of it?

426
00:24:17,501 --> 00:24:19,434
Government's the last resort.

427
00:24:20,313 --> 00:24:23,366
Why don't you just not buy games
that require an online connection,

428
00:24:23,367 --> 00:24:25,101
and vote with your wallet?

429
00:24:25,666 --> 00:24:28,426
Well for starters, we prefer
to vote with our votes.

430
00:24:28,426 --> 00:24:30,336
We think it's more democratic.

431
00:24:30,336 --> 00:24:33,706
But the main reason is, this
doesn't accomplish our goals.

432
00:24:33,706 --> 00:24:36,440
I mean, our goal is to
save games we like.

433
00:24:36,456 --> 00:24:38,886
So if we buy the
game, it gets destroyed.

434
00:24:38,886 --> 00:24:41,106
If we don't buy the
game, it gets destroyed.

435
00:24:41,107 --> 00:24:42,307
So...

436
00:24:42,706 --> 00:24:45,706
I mean, why don't you not
listen to music you like?

437
00:24:45,706 --> 00:24:48,573
Or why don't you not
watch movies you like?

438
00:24:48,676 --> 00:24:50,743
What exactly are we doing then?

439
00:24:51,296 --> 00:24:55,416
Of course, the real answer is, why
aren't we boycotting games that do this?

440
00:24:55,416 --> 00:24:56,700
Well, that's easy.

441
00:24:56,701 --> 00:25:01,346
To the best of my knowledge, I'm not
sure a boycott of a game has ever worked.

442
00:25:01,347 --> 00:25:02,547
Ever.

443
00:25:02,726 --> 00:25:05,593
And if it has, then
what I'm really sure of,

444
00:25:05,636 --> 00:25:09,866
is no game that's ENJOYABLE has
ever had a successful boycott.

445
00:25:10,336 --> 00:25:14,466
Like, I think the one for "Modern
Warfare 2" is a meme at this point.

446
00:25:14,466 --> 00:25:16,086
And boycotts have been tried.

447
00:25:16,086 --> 00:25:19,899
This is advocating for something
with a 100% failure rate.

448
00:25:20,386 --> 00:25:22,920
I would bet money
on that not working.

449
00:25:23,386 --> 00:25:26,866
What we're doing is trying something
that has never been done before,

450
00:25:26,916 --> 00:25:28,739
so it MIGHT work.

451
00:25:29,333 --> 00:25:30,733
Won't this go nowhere,

452
00:25:30,734 --> 00:25:34,200
because customers signed a EULA
that says companies can do this?

453
00:25:34,200 --> 00:25:35,433
A few things.

454
00:25:35,434 --> 00:25:37,700
Straight from the EU itself:

455
00:25:38,116 --> 00:25:42,333
"Directive 93/13/EEC
prohibits unfair terms

456
00:25:42,334 --> 00:25:45,071
"causing a significant
imbalance in the parties' rights

457
00:25:45,071 --> 00:25:47,900
"and obligations to the
detriment of consumers."

458
00:25:47,900 --> 00:25:52,000
Yeah, that's probably 95%
of all game EULAs ever written.

459
00:25:52,000 --> 00:25:55,033
That's being investigated
as you watch this.

460
00:25:55,034 --> 00:25:59,600
Plus, the initiative itself says it would
supersede the EULA on this issue.

461
00:25:59,600 --> 00:26:02,500
What we're doing here is trying
to take removal of your ownership

462
00:26:02,501 --> 00:26:04,181
off the table entirely.

463
00:26:04,390 --> 00:26:07,056
Would this initiative affect free games?

464
00:26:07,700 --> 00:26:12,033
Well, that depends. Is the game
truly "free", as in "freeware"?

465
00:26:12,033 --> 00:26:14,680
If yes, then no, it would be exempt.

466
00:26:14,730 --> 00:26:16,833
Do anything you want.

467
00:26:17,320 --> 00:26:20,607
But if it's "free-to-play" and
has microtransactions

468
00:26:20,607 --> 00:26:24,100
sold as one-time purchases
with no expiration date,

469
00:26:24,780 --> 00:26:26,833
then you're not
entitled to the game,

470
00:26:26,834 --> 00:26:30,240
but you are entitled to those
purchased microtransactions,

471
00:26:30,240 --> 00:26:33,040
which need the game
to function. So... yes.

472
00:26:33,866 --> 00:26:37,760
Doesn't this initiative intrude upon
developer rights and freedom?

473
00:26:37,760 --> 00:26:43,133
That is a valid argument to make,
and we respect it 100% for free games.

474
00:26:43,633 --> 00:26:46,733
But once you start taking
money to sell or lease something,

475
00:26:46,734 --> 00:26:48,867
you enter the world of commerce.

476
00:26:49,133 --> 00:26:50,466
And taking people's money

477
00:26:50,467 --> 00:26:54,179
means you're assuming more
responsibility for your actions.

478
00:26:54,250 --> 00:26:56,316
Every nation has rules on this.

479
00:26:56,733 --> 00:26:58,533
So, technically, yes,

480
00:26:58,750 --> 00:27:02,283
but that's to balance with
BUYER rights and freedoms.

481
00:27:02,666 --> 00:27:07,120
If a health inspector tells a restaurant
it needs to follow certain procedures,

482
00:27:07,120 --> 00:27:10,384
does that intrude upon a
chef's rights and freedoms?

483
00:27:10,510 --> 00:27:14,333
Yes, but as a society, we
generally think that's worth it.

484
00:27:14,770 --> 00:27:16,450
Now, that's about safety.

485
00:27:16,460 --> 00:27:19,667
This is about preservation
and consumer rights.

486
00:27:20,170 --> 00:27:22,600
Under your proposal,
when a game shuts down,

487
00:27:22,600 --> 00:27:25,393
does that mean everyone
gets the game for free?

488
00:27:25,393 --> 00:27:29,233
No, only existing customers
will be entitled to their copies.

489
00:27:29,566 --> 00:27:32,466
Now, that's less than
ideal for preservation,

490
00:27:32,543 --> 00:27:37,279
but to have anything else we'd have
to upend copyright law, and forget it.

491
00:27:37,393 --> 00:27:38,733
Besides, you never know.

492
00:27:38,733 --> 00:27:43,833
Some companies just release old games for
free anyway, but it would be their choice.

493
00:27:43,833 --> 00:27:47,033
What about people who are
banned from online games?

494
00:27:47,163 --> 00:27:51,693
Well, as long as the company is still
supporting the game, they still stay banned.

495
00:27:51,693 --> 00:27:52,966
However, as a customer,

496
00:27:52,967 --> 00:27:57,053
they would be entitled to an
end-of-life patch, whenever that was.

497
00:27:57,053 --> 00:28:01,983
So, it might be a year. It might be
15 years. Could be a pretty long ban.

498
00:28:02,600 --> 00:28:05,300
To have anything else would
create a loophole for developers,

499
00:28:05,301 --> 00:28:07,766
where they could just ban all
customers at the end of life,

500
00:28:07,767 --> 00:28:10,775
and skirt around the
requirement that way, so...

501
00:28:10,980 --> 00:28:13,880
Wouldn't this make
cheating rampant in games?

502
00:28:14,230 --> 00:28:17,466
You know, I've seen this question
before, and I'm always confused by it.

503
00:28:17,466 --> 00:28:19,390
So maybe I'm not
getting something here.

504
00:28:19,440 --> 00:28:22,600
Nothing would go into effect
until the game is shut down.

505
00:28:22,600 --> 00:28:26,166
So I don't know how that would
change anything compared to... now.

506
00:28:26,166 --> 00:28:30,669
Online games are under assault by cheaters
all the time, even without this initiative.

507
00:28:30,870 --> 00:28:34,750
Now, if you're talking about the potential
to cheat in games post-shutdown,

508
00:28:34,900 --> 00:28:37,950
then yes, cheating
could be very rampant.

509
00:28:38,150 --> 00:28:42,370
But why would that matter? Because the
alternative would be a completely dead game.

510
00:28:42,370 --> 00:28:45,710
Usually what happens in these
cases is small communities emerge,

511
00:28:45,711 --> 00:28:48,847
and they kind of self-police
to keep cheaters out.

512
00:28:49,066 --> 00:28:53,260
But yeah, I might be confused on
this question. I still don't quite get it.

513
00:28:53,260 --> 00:28:55,710
What if players purposefully
try to tank a game,

514
00:28:55,711 --> 00:28:59,070
knowing they can get an
end-of-life build if they do it?

515
00:28:59,070 --> 00:29:02,633
Well again, only paying customers
would be entitled to an end-of-life build,

516
00:29:02,633 --> 00:29:05,240
so they would still have
to pay for that privilege.

517
00:29:05,240 --> 00:29:09,144
But I think most people who buy
a game want to play that game.

518
00:29:09,220 --> 00:29:13,275
And the experience tends to be a
lot better while it is actively supported,

519
00:29:13,275 --> 00:29:15,460
so there's an incentive
to keep it going.

520
00:29:15,460 --> 00:29:18,510
But this is already a
risk of online games.

521
00:29:18,635 --> 00:29:21,230
I just mentioned earlier,
they're constantly under assault

522
00:29:21,231 --> 00:29:24,740
by people trying to force
cheats or exploits or whatever.

523
00:29:24,740 --> 00:29:27,633
I guess if it gets really
bad, you can sue.

524
00:29:27,634 --> 00:29:30,833
Bungie sued that cheat
maker, and they won.

525
00:29:30,834 --> 00:29:35,066
Plus, isn't there an entire
anti-cheat industry focused on this?

526
00:29:35,633 --> 00:29:37,866
I think what might happen
is you might see an increase

527
00:29:37,867 --> 00:29:41,168
of attempts of that at first
due to the novelty of it,

528
00:29:41,168 --> 00:29:44,748
but then incentive will probably
drop to where it is now already.

529
00:29:44,748 --> 00:29:47,548
What about console
games? Are they covered?

530
00:29:47,748 --> 00:29:49,428
This one gets tricky.

531
00:29:49,608 --> 00:29:54,216
The answer is yes, but it's another
one that depends on what the EU says.

532
00:29:54,233 --> 00:29:58,118
It might not be possible to release
the server on the console also,

533
00:29:58,118 --> 00:30:01,988
so what they would have to do is release
the server software for another platform

534
00:30:01,988 --> 00:30:03,468
that you could host elsewhere,

535
00:30:03,618 --> 00:30:07,478
and then be able to point the
console towards that separate server.

536
00:30:07,478 --> 00:30:10,188
But where it gets muddy is when
you need to pay the console maker

537
00:30:10,189 --> 00:30:12,908
just to have that internet
access in the first place.

538
00:30:13,058 --> 00:30:14,586
So there could still be liability,

539
00:30:14,586 --> 00:30:17,193
but it would be off the game
publishers shoulders at that point,

540
00:30:17,194 --> 00:30:19,058
and onto the console makers.

541
00:30:19,058 --> 00:30:21,900
And it's not clear to me if the
console maker would take liability

542
00:30:21,901 --> 00:30:25,933
when that game shuts down, or
when they discontinue the console.

543
00:30:26,133 --> 00:30:27,933
I think the latter
would be more likely,

544
00:30:27,934 --> 00:30:30,300
so the console maker would just
have to make it so the console

545
00:30:30,301 --> 00:30:33,393
is not bricked from the internet
once they discontinue it.

546
00:30:33,393 --> 00:30:36,903
So you would get your game
back, but it might take a while.

547
00:30:36,903 --> 00:30:40,000
What about mobile games?
Would they be affected?

548
00:30:40,533 --> 00:30:41,900
I don't see why not,

549
00:30:41,901 --> 00:30:46,623
though the sheer quantity of them might lend
more weight to the grandfathering argument.

550
00:30:46,623 --> 00:30:49,343
I think it would be a similar
situation as consoles.

551
00:30:49,343 --> 00:30:51,473
It either gets hosted
on the mobile device,

552
00:30:51,473 --> 00:30:54,566
or it's released for a separate
platform that it can connect to.

553
00:30:54,566 --> 00:30:58,790
How long would companies have
to host patches or end-of-life plans?

554
00:30:59,053 --> 00:31:02,813
I don't know, just long enough
so users have a chance to grab it?

555
00:31:02,813 --> 00:31:04,493
90 days? Could be less.

556
00:31:05,183 --> 00:31:09,855
Won't this mean companies stop making
MMO or live service games in the EU?

557
00:31:10,000 --> 00:31:13,593
I think it's a complete bluff from
any major company saying this.

558
00:31:13,743 --> 00:31:18,793
The population of the EU is 450
million people. It's a huge market.

559
00:31:18,993 --> 00:31:22,623
The industry stands to lose
billions by doing something like that.

560
00:31:22,623 --> 00:31:28,933
And remember, this doesn't exclude
microtransactions, DLC, even loot boxes,

561
00:31:29,103 --> 00:31:32,003
so companies could
keep the money rolling in.

562
00:31:32,573 --> 00:31:38,733
Now compare that loss to the cost of an end
of life plan from the design phase onward.

563
00:31:39,253 --> 00:31:41,786
I still think it could
be less than 1%.

564
00:31:42,300 --> 00:31:45,244
Companies tend to do what
makes the most money.

565
00:31:45,244 --> 00:31:47,528
But fine, let's say
they pulled out.

566
00:31:47,528 --> 00:31:51,318
You think other companies wouldn't
rush in to take their market share?

567
00:31:51,318 --> 00:31:54,633
The rules would have changed a little
bit, but the demand wouldn't have left.

568
00:31:54,733 --> 00:31:57,033
Even smaller companies
I think is also unlikely,

569
00:31:57,034 --> 00:32:00,303
especially as middleware
solutions for this would emerge.

570
00:32:00,303 --> 00:32:03,951
However, point three, the
in-between phase could be rocky.

571
00:32:04,353 --> 00:32:07,220
But to think that they're
going to go away?

572
00:32:07,366 --> 00:32:10,758
Yeah, I guess if companies
suddenly stop liking money.

573
00:32:11,003 --> 00:32:15,333
Wouldn't it be a security risk for
companies to release their server software?

574
00:32:15,333 --> 00:32:19,993
Mostly no, but there are some
cases yes, this is a valid argument.

575
00:32:20,193 --> 00:32:22,543
Particularly when a
company shuts down a game,

576
00:32:22,543 --> 00:32:25,433
but they have a new game
that runs on the same engine.

577
00:32:25,433 --> 00:32:30,193
So if users find exploits on the old engine,
they have them for the new one potentially.

578
00:32:30,193 --> 00:32:34,966
I swear I'm not trying to be dismissive, but
I have to call on points one and three again.

579
00:32:34,966 --> 00:32:38,906
So the question is, are these
outlier cases where this is a problem,

580
00:32:38,906 --> 00:32:42,298
a good enough excuse to
destroy all online-only games?

581
00:32:43,066 --> 00:32:44,986
Of course, I'm going to land on "no".

582
00:32:44,986 --> 00:32:47,520
The cost is too high
for everyone else.

583
00:32:48,033 --> 00:32:51,486
Is this only about single-player
games or multiplayer too?

584
00:32:51,486 --> 00:32:53,916
I'll answer that question with a question.

585
00:32:53,916 --> 00:32:56,466
What does "Destiny 2", "The Crew",

586
00:32:56,467 --> 00:33:00,099
"Need for Speed 2015", and "The
Division" all have in common?

587
00:33:00,696 --> 00:33:03,596
Are they single-player
or multiplayer games?

588
00:33:03,966 --> 00:33:05,166
Yes.

589
00:33:05,253 --> 00:33:09,093
So even if we didn't care about
multiplayer at all, which isn't the case,

590
00:33:09,094 --> 00:33:11,093
the industry has forced our hand.

591
00:33:11,106 --> 00:33:14,101
So in order to save
single-player in games like these,

592
00:33:14,101 --> 00:33:17,916
we have to save the multiplayer also
because they merged it all together.

593
00:33:17,916 --> 00:33:21,086
Otherwise, they would just get
exempt on almost every game.

594
00:33:21,086 --> 00:33:24,186
Plus an arena shooter
with eight players?

595
00:33:24,186 --> 00:33:25,486
Or 12?

596
00:33:25,486 --> 00:33:28,353
You can't have an
end-of-life plan for THAT?

597
00:33:28,401 --> 00:33:29,681
Give me a break.

598
00:33:29,921 --> 00:33:31,866
Now, MMOs can be a tall order,

599
00:33:31,867 --> 00:33:35,433
but we've seen both legitimate
and illegitimate emulators emerge

600
00:33:35,434 --> 00:33:37,266
to know this is possible.

601
00:33:37,266 --> 00:33:40,733
So it's both. The industry's
given us no choice.

602
00:33:41,033 --> 00:33:46,281
Would developers be required to convert
multiplayer games into single-player games?

603
00:33:46,336 --> 00:33:48,126
Now, this one kind
of confused me.

604
00:33:48,126 --> 00:33:51,196
I mean, nothing about the
gameplay would have to change.

605
00:33:51,196 --> 00:33:53,863
It's all about running
the game, period.

606
00:33:53,863 --> 00:33:56,926
Like, I'm thinking of a
case like "Starsiege: Tribes",

607
00:33:56,926 --> 00:33:59,086
where that's a
multiplayer-focused game.

608
00:33:59,086 --> 00:34:01,446
There's no bots,
campaign, or anything.

609
00:34:01,446 --> 00:34:05,542
But you can technically enter a
level and run around by yourself.

610
00:34:06,366 --> 00:34:08,266
Fine, mission accomplished.

611
00:34:08,536 --> 00:34:10,233
I guess you could
have a weird case,

612
00:34:10,234 --> 00:34:13,166
where you have a game that's
meant for co-op or something,

613
00:34:13,166 --> 00:34:17,966
and the game won't start unless both
players are connected at the same time.

614
00:34:19,036 --> 00:34:23,286
As long as it's possible to run it for
those two players or however many,

615
00:34:23,286 --> 00:34:28,371
then yeah, it still works, so I don't see any
reason they would really have to change.

616
00:34:28,371 --> 00:34:31,271
Wouldn't more clear
labeling fix the problem?

617
00:34:31,466 --> 00:34:34,333
Well, the problem is
games being destroyed.

618
00:34:34,886 --> 00:34:38,033
So I kind of don't care how
clearly the game is telling me

619
00:34:38,034 --> 00:34:40,266
it's going to destroy
a game I paid for.

620
00:34:40,266 --> 00:34:42,876
I don't like that being on
the table to begin with.

621
00:34:42,876 --> 00:34:46,780
Now, the exception is a hard date
when the game stops working.

622
00:34:46,906 --> 00:34:52,028
Like subscription games. You stop paying
after 30 days, that game ends for you.

623
00:34:52,032 --> 00:34:54,853
So I think if people saw on
the box or the store page

624
00:34:54,854 --> 00:34:59,200
that "this game expires
on January 1st, 2026",

625
00:34:59,666 --> 00:35:03,500
I think that would wake people
up and change their buying habits.

626
00:35:03,500 --> 00:35:06,700
But I also think anything
less than that would not.

627
00:35:07,233 --> 00:35:09,396
This becomes a very
psychological thing then,

628
00:35:09,397 --> 00:35:13,621
and it's a way riskier route to
saving games than what we're doing.

629
00:35:13,856 --> 00:35:15,590
How will this be enforced?

630
00:35:16,233 --> 00:35:18,556
I don't know. Ask
the European Union.

631
00:35:18,556 --> 00:35:22,596
I mean, how does it work now if a business
does something like false advertising?

632
00:35:22,596 --> 00:35:26,036
Do they get fined? Maybe a percentage
of their earnings? I don't know.

633
00:35:26,186 --> 00:35:27,546
That's not our job.

634
00:35:27,546 --> 00:35:31,596
I do hope the penalty will be big
enough to be a deterrent, however.

635
00:35:31,596 --> 00:35:36,016
Why don't you just petition companies
doing this instead of making a new law?

636
00:35:36,016 --> 00:35:38,283
No offense, but how naive are you?

637
00:35:38,666 --> 00:35:41,733
I mean, I guess that
might work 1% of the time.

638
00:35:42,026 --> 00:35:46,076
I mean, the companies open to
that idea tend to do it on their own.

639
00:35:46,076 --> 00:35:49,516
I mean, right now we're facing
hundreds of games at risk.

640
00:35:49,516 --> 00:35:54,220
So what makes more sense? To petition
every single company behind those games?

641
00:35:54,506 --> 00:35:56,916
Or to do it once to
the government?

642
00:35:58,400 --> 00:36:01,800
What versions of the game
will the dev have to release?

643
00:36:01,800 --> 00:36:05,696
This is another one that's hazy.
I personally would argue "any".

644
00:36:05,696 --> 00:36:07,830
It just gets so muddy otherwise.

645
00:36:07,900 --> 00:36:11,496
For most games, the latest
version tends to be the best one.

646
00:36:11,496 --> 00:36:17,128
But not always, and a few games tend to have
big differences in content between versions.

647
00:36:17,146 --> 00:36:19,656
So I can understand people
wanting to go back, but...

648
00:36:19,656 --> 00:36:22,536
Man, that might be a
bridge too far to ask on this.

649
00:36:22,536 --> 00:36:25,406
I guess I should have added
this to the compromise list.

650
00:36:25,406 --> 00:36:28,350
Won't games get more
expensive because of this?

651
00:36:28,376 --> 00:36:33,176
I'll keep it real. It's not impossible,
but it's probably not why you think.

652
00:36:33,406 --> 00:36:38,014
Okay, for sake of argument, let's
say it costs 1% of the total game cost.

653
00:36:38,106 --> 00:36:41,526
And let's also say a new
$70 game just breaks even.

654
00:36:41,526 --> 00:36:43,499
So 1% of that cost.

655
00:36:43,676 --> 00:36:45,466
That will put it at $70.70.

656
00:36:45,466 --> 00:36:47,156
But what's to stop a company from saying,

657
00:36:47,157 --> 00:36:49,900
"Hey, we have to conform
to end-of-life plans now,"

658
00:36:49,901 --> 00:36:53,767
"so why don't we really push
the price to $75? Or $80?"

659
00:36:54,106 --> 00:36:56,033
"Hell, let's try for $85."

660
00:36:56,435 --> 00:36:58,902
And yes, some
companies could do that.

661
00:36:59,406 --> 00:37:02,666
And this sort of thing has already
happened outside of games.

662
00:37:02,666 --> 00:37:05,606
Like after the pandemic,
we had a lot of inflation.

663
00:37:05,606 --> 00:37:07,340
Let's just say it was 10%.

664
00:37:07,546 --> 00:37:11,000
So instead of raising their prices 10%,
companies looked at that and thought,

665
00:37:11,001 --> 00:37:14,666
"Why don't we raise them
50%? Or 100% or 200%?"

666
00:37:14,666 --> 00:37:18,746
"We can make a lot more money then,
and we can blame it all on inflation."

667
00:37:18,746 --> 00:37:22,476
So there was real inflation AND
companies jacked up the price.

668
00:37:22,476 --> 00:37:24,776
But the thing is, you either
have to own the market

669
00:37:24,776 --> 00:37:27,386
or do that collectively
with other companies.

670
00:37:27,386 --> 00:37:30,186
Because as soon as one
big company breaks ranks,

671
00:37:30,186 --> 00:37:32,233
then they can kind of get
away with more marketshare

672
00:37:32,234 --> 00:37:34,001
because they're selling
their game cheaper.

673
00:37:34,001 --> 00:37:38,166
I'm honestly not sure if there are
price-fixing laws on this sort of thing.

674
00:37:38,166 --> 00:37:41,366
Isn't Sony being sued over
jacking up their prices?

675
00:37:42,033 --> 00:37:43,666
The thing to remember
is, they're going to try and

676
00:37:43,667 --> 00:37:46,467
find ways to increase
prices on you anyway.

677
00:37:46,506 --> 00:37:50,051
So is fear of a price increase,
which they may or may not do,

678
00:37:50,051 --> 00:37:53,366
which may get them into trouble
with regulatory boards anyway,

679
00:37:53,366 --> 00:37:56,266
worth the cost of
destroying all online games?

680
00:37:56,966 --> 00:37:58,166
I'd say no.

681
00:37:58,866 --> 00:38:01,499
What if a game
company goes bankrupt?

682
00:38:01,500 --> 00:38:04,200
I mean, the most likely
penalties would be financial,

683
00:38:04,201 --> 00:38:07,033
so if they're bankrupt,
then it's kind of moot point.

684
00:38:07,033 --> 00:38:10,903
Yeah, the game would still die, but then the
company would have more skin in the game.

685
00:38:10,903 --> 00:38:15,831
There wouldn't be a financial incentive
to kill the game the way there is now.

686
00:38:15,903 --> 00:38:20,103
I would think most companies are
very motivated not to go bankrupt.

687
00:38:20,103 --> 00:38:22,566
Hey, if every time a game
had been destroyed in the past,

688
00:38:22,567 --> 00:38:25,033
the parent company had
gone bankrupt over it,

689
00:38:25,033 --> 00:38:27,373
I wouldn't have even
started this campaign.

690
00:38:27,373 --> 00:38:29,800
Won't big companies just
make shell companies,

691
00:38:29,801 --> 00:38:33,168
so they bankrupt the company every
time they want to shut down a game?

692
00:38:33,168 --> 00:38:35,093
Again, it's not impossible,

693
00:38:35,093 --> 00:38:37,893
but remember, companies
like to make money.

694
00:38:38,133 --> 00:38:43,093
So is the cost of shuttering an entire studio
and cooking the books to make them bankrupt

695
00:38:43,093 --> 00:38:48,633
and spinning up a new studio for the next
game more than having an end-of-life plan?

696
00:38:48,983 --> 00:38:53,023
Plus, this would make having a
good reputation kind of impossible.

697
00:38:53,023 --> 00:38:57,863
What about games that are so huge in scope
they can't be hosted on home computers?

698
00:38:57,863 --> 00:39:01,793
Well, I think this leads more
credence to the best effort solution.

699
00:39:01,793 --> 00:39:05,683
I can think of two examples.
One is "EVE Online".

700
00:39:05,683 --> 00:39:10,300
I believe it can host up to half
a million players simultaneously.

701
00:39:11,083 --> 00:39:14,288
Yeah, no customer system's
going to be able to do that.

702
00:39:14,288 --> 00:39:15,703
But if they had to shut down,

703
00:39:15,703 --> 00:39:17,566
does that mean they can't
release an end-of-life build

704
00:39:17,567 --> 00:39:19,901
where maybe a
hundred could connect?

705
00:39:20,163 --> 00:39:22,183
Or maybe they just
release the server code,

706
00:39:22,183 --> 00:39:25,393
let the community figure out, and they
decide how many custom resources

707
00:39:25,394 --> 00:39:27,653
they want to throw at
this for how many players.

708
00:39:27,653 --> 00:39:31,193
Another case I'm thinking of
is "Microsoft Flight Simulator",

709
00:39:31,193 --> 00:39:35,118
where in order for that game to look
its best, it has to connect to Bing Maps,

710
00:39:35,118 --> 00:39:38,062
which has two petabytes
of data it's accessing.

711
00:39:38,373 --> 00:39:40,573
So if that game had to shut down,

712
00:39:40,663 --> 00:39:44,128
I think it would be reasonable for
them to say, "Okay, here's all the data."

713
00:39:44,128 --> 00:39:46,773
"We'll keep it hosted
for 90 days or so."

714
00:39:46,773 --> 00:39:49,934
"Good luck trying to download
it. You're on your own now."

715
00:39:49,934 --> 00:39:54,030
As long as it's possible in some
capacity, that's the main point.

716
00:39:54,263 --> 00:39:56,933
The players can deal
with all the rough edges.

717
00:39:56,933 --> 00:39:59,066
Don't some games deserve to die?

718
00:39:59,453 --> 00:40:00,666
Actually, yes.

719
00:40:01,033 --> 00:40:03,753
But one man's trash is
another man's treasure.

720
00:40:03,753 --> 00:40:06,020
So that's a subjective assessment.

721
00:40:06,203 --> 00:40:08,343
If you've paid money into the game,

722
00:40:08,343 --> 00:40:11,873
then you should have a voice on
whether you get to keep it or not.

723
00:40:11,873 --> 00:40:14,503
If all online games are awful,

724
00:40:14,503 --> 00:40:16,933
then yeah, this initiative
wouldn't be necessary.

725
00:40:16,933 --> 00:40:18,900
But that's not reality.

726
00:40:19,873 --> 00:40:23,333
How does Ubisoft and "The
Crew" relate to this initiative?

727
00:40:23,333 --> 00:40:26,153
That's completely
separate from this initiative.

728
00:40:26,154 --> 00:40:28,820
That was about reporting to
consumer protection agencies,

729
00:40:28,821 --> 00:40:30,553
and that's largely underway.

730
00:40:30,553 --> 00:40:31,833
So those are two different things.

731
00:40:31,834 --> 00:40:34,933
You don't have to be connected
to "The Crew" for any of this.

732
00:40:34,933 --> 00:40:37,833
What are the chances
of this initiative passing?

733
00:40:37,833 --> 00:40:39,623
That's a hard question to answer.

734
00:40:39,623 --> 00:40:43,653
I mean, for getting the signatures...
you tell me, I don't know.

735
00:40:43,653 --> 00:40:47,557
I would bet if we do get the
signatures, this solves problems.

736
00:40:47,738 --> 00:40:50,178
But that's almost not
the way to even look at it.

737
00:40:50,178 --> 00:40:51,433
It's like I said at
the beginning,

738
00:40:51,434 --> 00:40:55,433
the Initiative and Stop Killing Games 
is IT for action on this issue.

739
00:40:55,433 --> 00:40:57,863
If we fail at this, we fail for life.

740
00:40:57,863 --> 00:41:01,063
The industry is only going to get
worse permanently on this issue.

741
00:41:01,063 --> 00:41:03,813
I feel like the Dutch boy with
my finger in the dam here.

742
00:41:03,813 --> 00:41:07,397
If this goes, that's it. There's
no round two. It's over.

743
00:41:07,493 --> 00:41:11,381
Okay, that's it for questions.
I have a few quick corrections.

744
00:41:11,700 --> 00:41:14,953
First, I made a mistake
on this video with counting,

745
00:41:14,953 --> 00:41:18,043
where I said your votes don't count
unless they pass the threshold.

746
00:41:18,043 --> 00:41:21,819
No, they do. So don't worry
about that. Just get more votes.

747
00:41:22,383 --> 00:41:26,466
The second is for some
countries, you can use EID.

748
00:41:26,743 --> 00:41:29,533
The way it was explained to me
is if you already have one of these,

749
00:41:29,534 --> 00:41:31,063
you don't need our guides.

750
00:41:31,063 --> 00:41:34,393
So we left it out just to keep
it simple, but you can use it.

751
00:41:34,393 --> 00:41:37,466
Okay, well, there are probably
literally endless questions,

752
00:41:37,467 --> 00:41:41,499
but I hope I answered enough
to clear up where we stand on this.

753
00:41:41,833 --> 00:41:44,433
I have put some
thought into this topic.

754
00:41:44,443 --> 00:41:47,493
This video hopefully got
rid of some fence sitters.

755
00:41:47,493 --> 00:41:50,226
Which side they landed
on, I have no idea.

756
00:41:50,293 --> 00:41:52,360
For those on board, go sign it.

757
00:41:52,893 --> 00:41:54,760
All right, that's it.
See ya!

758
00:41:57,273 --> 00:42:05,273
[BLANK_AUDIO]

